.Dd October 18, 2026
.Dt SCREEN-MIRROR 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm screen-mirror
.Nd mirror one X11 output onto the others
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl knv
.Op Fl m Ar mode
.Op Fl s Ar source
.Op Ar target ...
.Nm
.Op Fl s Ar source
.Fl l
.Nm
.Op Fl nv
.Op Fl s Ar source
.Fl x
.Nm
.Fl h
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
mirrors one output onto every other connected output, under X11, on
.Fx
and Linux alike.
The source is the primary output, unless
.Fl s
names another
.Pq or the first connected output, when none is primary ;
the targets are every other connected output, unless some are named as
operands.
.Pp
Picking the mode is the whole job.
.Ql xrandr --auto
is not it: a projector or a cheap HDMI switch commonly advertises
1024x768 as its preferred mode, so
.Ql --auto
mirrors at 1024x768.
By default every output taking part is set to the largest mode they
all list, which is an exact, pixel-for-pixel mirror, but can pull the
internal panel below its native resolution
.Pq 1920x1200 to 1920x1080, against a 1080p projector .
.Fl k
keeps the source at its own mode and scales each target to it instead,
at the cost of a resampled, possibly stretched image on the target.
When no mode is common to every output,
.Fl k
is used whether it was asked for or not, and
.Nm
says so.
.Pp
Every question
.Nm
asks \(em which outputs are connected, which is primary, what modes
each lists \(em is answered from a single
.Ql xrandr --query ,
so all the answers describe the same instant; and the change is then
one
.Xr xrandr 1
call, so the screens never pass through a half-applied layout.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width "-s source"
.It Fl h
Print the usage and exit.
.It Fl k
Keep the source at its mode
.Pq Fl m Ns 's, else the one it drives now, else its preferred one ,
and drive each target at its own preferred mode with the source's
picture scaled onto it.
.It Fl l
List the connected outputs, each with its preferred mode, the mode it
drives now and how many modes it lists, then the modes they all share,
and exit.
.It Fl m Ar mode
Use this mode, as
.Ar W Ns x Ns Ar H ,
instead of picking one.
The source must list it, and so must every target unless
.Fl k .
.It Fl n
Dry run: print the
.Xr xrandr 1
command instead of running it.
.It Fl s Ar source
The output to mirror, instead of the primary.
It must be connected.
.It Fl v
Report the outputs found, the source, the targets and the mode chosen,
on stderr.
.It Fl x
Undo: turn the targets off, and set the source back to its preferred
mode, unscaled, at 0x0, as the primary.
.El
.Pp
.Fl l
and
.Fl x
exclude each other, and take no
.Ar target .
A
.Ar target
named must be connected, and must not be the source.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width TMPDIR
.It Ev TMPDIR
Where the probe is kept while
.Nm
runs, instead of
.Pa /tmp .
.It Ev XRANDR
The
.Xr xrandr 1
command to run, instead of
.Ql xrandr .
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It 0
The outputs were mirrored, listed or put back, or the command was
printed with
.Fl n .
.It 1
No output is connected, there is nothing to mirror to, an output named
is not connected, or a mode is not listed or there is none to use.
.It 2
A usage error, a
.Ar mode
not of the form
.Ar W Ns x Ns Ar H
included.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Mirror the laptop's panel onto a projector, at the largest mode both
list:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ screen-mirror
.Ed
.Pp
Keep the panel at its native resolution and scale the projector's
picture to it:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ screen-mirror -k
.Ed
.Pp
See what the outputs share, then go back to the panel alone:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ screen-mirror -l
$ screen-mirror -x
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr screen-extend 1 ,
.Xr xrandr 1
.Sh CAVEATS
.Nm
works under X11 only; a Wayland session has no
.Xr xrandr 1
to drive.
.Pp
.Fl x
is the quick way back to one screen;
.Xr screen-extend 1
lays the outputs out side by side instead.
